/P 43 0 R
/T (Transfer learning initializes models with weights pretrained on large image corpora \(typically ImageNet\) before fine-tuning on medical data, partially mitigating the small-dataset problem endemic to neuroimaging. AlSaeed and Omar [4] demonstrated that ResNet-50's deep residual feature maps, when combined with a Softmax classifier, achieve 99% accuracy on ADNI MRI data, outperforming the same backbone paired with SVM or Random Forest classifiers. This finding that the choice of classification head matters as much as the backbone informs the hybrid experiments described in Section 2.3. Sava [10] conducted the most systematic transfer-learning comparison in this corpus, evaluating twenty-nine pretrained architectures on the Kaggle AD dataset. EfficientNetB0 emerged as the top performer at 92.98% test accuracy, with EfficientNetB2 and EfficientNetB3 offering superior precision and sensitivity. The EfficientNet family's compound scaling  balancing depth, width, and resolution simultaneously  proves advantageous over uniform depth-scaling strategies such as VGG or plain ResNets. Nevertheless, the study does not integrate any explainability technique, and the accuracy gap relative to custom CNNs \(92.98% vs. 99.22%\) suggests that ImageNet pretraining is not universally superior for medical imaging, particularly when domain-specific augmentation is available. Naz et al. [12] evaluated several standard DNN architectures on the Kaggle MRI four-class task, finding that DenseNet-121 achieved the best average performance across accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. DenseNet's dense connectivity  where each layer receives feature maps from all preceding layers  promotes feature reuse and gradient flow, which is especially beneficial when training data is limited. The limitation is that dense connectivity increases memory usage substantially relative to ResNet or EfficientNet variants. Mohammed et al. [9] took a hybrid approach, pairing AlexNet feature extraction with an SVM classifier, achieving 94.8% accuracy and a remarkably high AUC of 99.70% on the Kaggle dataset. While AlexNet is architecturally dated, the strong AUC result suggests that SVM boundary optimisation can effectively)
/E (Transfer learning initializes models with weights pretrained on large image corpora \(typically ImageNet\) before fine-tuning on medical data, partially mitigating the small-dataset problem endemic to neuroimaging. AlSaeed and Omar [4] demonstrated that ResNet-50's deep residual feature maps, when combined with a Softmax classifier, achieve 99% accuracy on ADNI MRI data, outperforming the same backbone paired with SVM or Random Forest classifiers. This finding that the choice of classification head matters as much as the backbone informs the hybrid experiments described in Section 2.3. Sava [10] conducted the most systematic transfer-learning comparison in this corpus, evaluating twenty-nine pretrained architectures on the Kaggle AD dataset. EfficientNetB0 emerged as the top performer at 92.98% test accuracy, with EfficientNetB2 and EfficientNetB3 offering superior precision and sensitivity. The EfficientNet family's compound scaling  balancing depth, width, and resolution simultaneously  proves advantageous over uniform depth-scaling strategies such as VGG or plain ResNets. Nevertheless, the study does not integrate any explainability technique, and the accuracy gap relative to custom CNNs \(92.98% vs. 99.22%\) suggests that ImageNet pretraining is not universally superior for medical imaging, particularly when domain-specific augmentation is available. Naz et al. [12] evaluated several standard DNN architectures on the Kaggle MRI four-class task, finding that DenseNet-121 achieved the best average performance across accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. DenseNet's dense connectivity  where each layer receives feature maps from all preceding layers  promotes feature reuse and gradient flow, which is especially beneficial when training data is limited. The limitation is that dense connectivity increases memory usage substantially relative to ResNet or EfficientNet variants. Mohammed et al. [9] took a hybrid approach, pairing AlexNet feature extraction with an SVM classifier, achieving 94.8% accuracy and a remarkably high AUC of 99.70% on the Kaggle dataset. While AlexNet is architecturally dated, the strong AUC result suggests that SVM boundary optimisation can effectively)
