240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (This review has examined twelve peer-reviewed, model-based studies on deep learning for Alzheimer's disease classification, comparing their architectures, training strategies, class-imbalance handling, dataset choices, and explainability integration. The analysis reveals a clear progression from simple custom CNNs through hybrid deep-classical pipelines to multimodal and transfer-learning frameworks, with accuracy consistently above 99% for the best configurations on both ADNI and Kaggle datasets. However, accuracy alone is an insufficient criterion for clinical adoption: the near-universal absence of XAI mechanisms across reviewed papers represents the most critical unaddressed gap in the field.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 47 0 R
/T (8. Conclusion)
/E (8. Conclusion)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 47 0 R
/E (The proposed framework addresses this gap by combining the strongest hybrid architecture from the reviewed literature \(SECNN-RF [1]\) with four complementary XAI techniques applied to the equalised Kaggle four-class dataset. By evaluating multiple model families under identical conditions and validating explanations against established AD biomarkers, this study is designed to advance both diagnostic accuracy and clinical interpretability demonstrating that technical performance and transparency are not competing objectives but mutually reinforcing ones.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
