>>
endobj
649 0 obj
<<
/Title (The reviewed literature reveals four persistent gaps. First, the majority of high-accuracy models including AbdulAzeem et al. [2], El-Latif et al. [5], Ahmed et al. [3], and Basaia et al. [8] report no explainability mechanism whatsoever, making it impossible for clinicians to verify the anatomical grounding of predictions. This is particularly problematic given that some reported accuracies \(above 99%\) may reflect dataset-specific overfitting rather than genuine generalisation. Second, where hybrid architectures exist \(e.g., CAD-ALZ [7], HTLML [11], AlexNet+SVM [9], ResNet-50+Softmax [4]\), they are evaluated in isolation rather than systematically compared against end-to-end alternatives on the same dataset and under the same experimental conditions. This makes it difficult to attribute performance differences to the classifier choice versus the feature extractor versus the training strategy. Third, the treatment of class imbalance is inconsistent across the literature. DAD-Net [3] demonstrates that ADASYN oversampling alone can close a ten-point accuracy gap, yet most other reviewed papers apply no explicit balancing strategy and report inflated accuracy on majority-class predictions. The present study's use of the equalised Kaggle dataset, combined with class-weighted loss functions, addresses this more rigorously than any single reviewed paper. Fourth, no reviewed paper comprehensively combines a high-accuracy hybrid pipeline with multiple complementary XAI techniques validated against known AD biomarkers. AbdelAziz et al. [1] comes closest by integrating SHAP into SECNN-RF, but relies on a single XAI method and does not compare its explanations against clinical literature systematically. In response to these gaps, the present study proposes an integrated framework that: \(i\) evaluates multiple model families  custom CNNs [2, 3, 5], hybrid pipelines [1, 7, 11], transfer-learning backbones [4, 9, 10, 12], and multimodal approaches [6 ,8] under identical experimental conditions on the equalized Kaggle dataset; \(ii\) applies class-weighted loss functions to guard against residual imbalance; \(iii\) integrates Grad-CAM, SHAP, LIME, and Integrated Gradients to provide multi-perspective model interpretation; and \(iv\) validates highlighted regions against established AD neuropathology. This combination of breadth, rigor, and interpretability distinguishes the present study from all individually reviewed works.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 616 0 R
>>
endobj
